UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 11, Lesson 2: Natural and built features', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Natural features exist without people, such as creeks, hills, beaches and bush. Built features are made by people, such as roads, bridges and benches. Some places are managed, such as a planted garden, where people shape living things. Built features can affect natural ones.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same HSIE idea to a new place or source, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nSort: waterfall, dam, footpath, bush, a pond dug by council. Which is hardest to place and why?\n\nFEEDBACK\nNatural: waterfall and bush. Built: dam, footpath and dug pond. The pond is hardest because it holds living plants even though people made it.\n\nWATCH FOR\nBelieving that anything green is natural ignores that people plant and shape gardens, lawns and sports fields.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nMake a two-column table of eight natural and eight built features from a place you know. Mark two where people have shaped living things. Write one sentence on how a built feature affects a natural one.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one source or idea you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"HSIE","learning_intention":"Natural features exist without people, such as creeks, hills, beaches and bush. Built features are made by people, such as roads, bridges and benches. Some places are managed, such as a planted garden, where people shape living things. Built features can affect natural ones.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Apply and respond","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: local map, photo or interview notes"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["HS2-GEO-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Make a two-column table of eight natural and eight built features from a place you know. Mark two where people have shaped living things. Write one sentence on how a built feature affects a natural one. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Natural: waterfall and bush. Built: dam, footpath and dug pond. The pond is hardest because it holds living plants even though people made it. Reasoning: I used natural by attending to built and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Believing that anything green is natural ignores that people plant and shape gardens, lawns and sports fields. Correction: Natural: waterfall and bush. Built: dam, footpath and dug pond. The pond is hardest because it holds living plants even though people made it. Why: the correction restores accurate use of natural.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Believing that anything green is natural ignores that people plant and shape gardens, lawns and sports fields. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating natural on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with natural, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Natural: waterfall and bush. Built: dam, footpath and dug pond. The pond is hardest because it holds living plants even though people made it. This matches the lesson skill for natural.","options":["Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","Natural: waterfall and bush. Built: dam, footpath and dug pond. The pond is hardest because it holds living plants even though people made it.","Believing that anything green is natural ignores that people plant and shape gardens, lawns and sports fields."],"question":"Sort: waterfall, dam, footpath, bush, a pond dug by council. Which is hardest to place and why?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Believing that anything green is natural ignores that people plant and shape gardens, lawns and sports fields. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Natural features exist without people, such as creeks, hills, beaches and bush. Built features are made by people, such as roads, bridges and benches. Some…","Natural: waterfall and bush. Built: dam, footpath and dug pond. The pond is hardest because it holds living plants even though people made it.","Careful use of built and explain why it matters with clear evidence.","Believing that anything green is natural ignores that people plant and shape gardens, lawns and sports fields."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising natural?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named built and explain why it matters, not by copying the model.","options":["Apply the idea with new evidence and name the built and explain why it matters used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a missing detail as a proven fact."],"question":"A learner is practising natural. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Natural: waterfall and bush. Built: dam, footpath and dug pond. The pond is hardest because it holds living plants even though people made it. uses sources and places appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","Natural: waterfall and bush. Built: dam, footpath and dug pond. The pond is hardest because it holds living plants even though people made it.","Believing that anything green is natural ignores that people plant and shape gardens, lawns and sports fields."],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about natural?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail.","Believing that anything green is natural ignores that people plant and shape gardens, lawns and sports fields.","Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about natural.","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about built and explain why it matters?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Example: at a beach, sand dunes and rock pools are natural. The surf club, seawall and boardwalk are built. Planted dune grass is managed…","Believing that anything green is natural ignores that people plant and shape gardens, lawns and sports fields.","Treat a missing detail as a proven fact.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Natural features exist without people, such as creeks, hills, beaches and bush. Built features are made by… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete.","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every online claim as equally reliable."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Believing that anything green is natural ignores that people plant and shape gardens, lawns and sports fields.","Make a two-column table of eight natural and eight built features from a place you know. Mark two where people have shaped living things. Write one…","Retype the worked example with no new example.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work."],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of natural?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating natural on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Natural: waterfall and bush. Built: dam, footpath and dug pond. The pond is hardest because it holds living plants even though people made it. Reasoning: I used natural by attending to built and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with natural, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Believing that anything green is natural ignores that people plant and shape gardens, lawns and sports fields. Correction: Natural: waterfall and bush. Built: dam, footpath and dug pond. The pond is hardest because it holds living plants even though people made it. Why: the correction restores accurate use of natural.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-hsie-y3-w11-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA HSIE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (HS2-*); content-point mapping and local First Nations protocols remain pending human review.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise natural.","Read the mission before you start mapping or using sources.","Keep the place, people or source you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the source or place detail that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore sources or respectful practice.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread names, places, dates and sequence.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact source detail or place feature before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key evidence.","Say what each source or place detail shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or use of sources, not only how neat the poster looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, person or source that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new sources or places."]},"subject":"Geography","success_criteria":["I can use natural with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify built and explain why it matters.","I can practise managed independently on a new prompt.","I can notice impact needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Natural features exist without people, such as creeks, hills, beaches and bush. Built features are made by people, such as roads, bridges and benches. Some places are managed, such as a planted garden, where people shape living things. Built features can affect natural ones.…","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence.","Natural features exist without people, such as creeks, hills, beaches and bush. Built features are made by people, such as roads, bridges and benches. Some places are managed, such as a planted garden, where people shape living things. Built features can affect natural ones.","A claim unrelated to the source or place."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about natural?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: at a beach, sand dunes and rock pools are natural. The surf club, seawall and boardwalk are built. Planted dune grass is managed because people planted it to hold the sand.\n\nTRY FIRST\nSort: waterfall, dam, footpath, bush, a pond dug by council. Which is hardest to place and why?\n\nFEEDBACK\nNatural: waterfall and bush. Built: dam, footpath and dug pond. The pond is hardest because it holds living plants even though people made it.","explain":"Natural features exist without people, such as creeks, hills, beaches and bush. Built features are made by people, such as roads, bridges and benches. Some places are managed, such as a planted garden, where people shape living things. Built features can affect natural ones.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Believing that anything green is natural ignores that people plant and shape gardens, lawns and sports fields. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Natural and built features at a beach.","caption":"Sort by who made it.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Natural</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">dunes, rock pools</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Built</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">surf club, seawall</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which source or place detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Example: at a beach, sand dunes and rock pools are natural. The surf club, seawall and boardwalk are built. Planted dune Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which source or place detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Example: at a beach, sand dunes and rock pools are natural. The surf club, seawall and boardwalk are built. Planted dune Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence.","Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about natural?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: at a beach, sand dunes and rock pools are natural. The surf club, seawall and boardwalk are built. Planted dune grass is managed because people planted it to hold the sand. Look at each observation, source detail or example that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show HSIE thinking. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Managed places and their effects.","caption":"Green is not always natural.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Managed</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">planted dune grass</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Impact</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">boardwalk protects dunes</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copi